(965, '-pi´#2', '-pi´', 'pi', 'pI', 'older sister', 'hermana mayor', NULL),
(432, '-pIkA', '-pIcA', 'pica', 'pIkA', 'flow', 'manar', NULL),
(433, '-pïki#1', '-pïqui', 'piqui', 'pIkI', 'invent', 'inventar', NULL),
(968, '-pïki#2', '-pïqui', 'piqui', 'pIkI', 'close', 'cerrar', 'Evidence for the length of the vowels comes from the modern dialect in\r\nMecayapan.\r\n'),
(79, '-pil#2', '-pil', 'pil', 'pIl', 'child', 'hijo', 'The sequence pil has a variety of different meanings; it appears compounded\r\nwith other roots, as an affix, and also independently.\r\nSo far we have distributed the occurrences on two roots and an affix. The two\r\nroots are pil ´hang´ and pil ´child´. In identifying the word pilli ´child´\r\nwith the word pilli ´noble´ we simply follow the tradition.\r\nHowever, when we identify pil in the word metlapilli ´mano de metate´ with\r\nthe root for ´child´, we base it on the observation that in other Mesoamerican\r\nlanguages the word for ´mano de metate´ has the meaning ´child of the metate´,\r\nthis is true of various Mayan languages, some Mixe languages, Zapotec, and\r\nprobably others.\r\nWe have no similar argument for the words mapilli ´finger and xopilli ´toe´.\r\nIn the word cuitlapilli ´tail´ we consider the sequence pil an affix, but not\r\nwith much conviction; concerning the word nenepilli ´tongue´ we have put off\r\nthe decision; and in topilli (both vowels are long) the sequence is part of\r\nthe root topil.\r\nA comparison of the words in various modern dialects does not help much; it\r\nshows variation as to vowel length (long vowel is here indicated with a\r\ncolon, glottal stop with ´, and in some dialects with j):\r\n\r\n            child  mano de m.    tail         tongue          finger\r\n\r\nCuatl.      -pil   metlapi:lli  cuitlapi:lli   nenepi:lli     macpilli\r\nSan Pedro   pil-   metlapi:l    i:-cuitlapi:l  no-nenepi:l    no-mahpil\r\nIxhuatl.    pil-   mitlapille   i:-cotlapi:l   no-nepi:l      no-ma´pil\r\nTetelc.     pil    metlapi:li   i:-cuitlapil   i:-nenepil     i:-majpil\r\n  Lastra:          metlapele !                 nenepile\r\nXalitla            metlapi:hli  cuitlapi:hli   nenepihli      ma:pihli\r\nZacap.      -pil                i:-cuitapil    i:-nenepil     i:-majpil\r\nMecayap.           me:metapil   i-cuitapil                    i-majpil\r\nZitlala            metlapilli   cuitlapi:lli   nenepi:lli\r\nQuacuila           ma:metlatl                  nenepi:li      ma´pili\r\nCoatepec C.  -pil  melapili     i:-cuitlapil   nu-nenepi:l    i:-ma´pil\r\nCarochi      -pil                              -nenepil       -ma´pil\r\n                                                  2 exx.        2 exx.\r\n'),
(552, '-pil#1', '-pil', 'pil', 'pIl', 'hang', 'colgar', NULL),
(553, '-pili', '-pili', 'pili', 'pIlI', 'wither', 'marchitarse', 'Evidence for the length of the first vowel is broadly attested in modern\r\ndialects, Huazalinguillo, Mecayapan, Tetelcingo, etc. where ´wither´ is\r\npilïni, however, the second vowel appears to be lengthened by the suffix ni;\r\nin the dialects of Coatepec C., Pipil, Zitlala, etc. we find a form with the\r\nsuffix ch(a), pilichahui ´be wrinkled´ in which the second vowel is short.\r\n'),
(877, '-pilö', '-pilö', 'pilo', 'pIlO', 'nephew of woman', 'sobrino de mujer', 'Evidence for the length of the vowels comes from one modern dialect are, La\r\nHuasteca.\r\n'),
(1078, '-pin', '-pin', 'pin', 'pIn', 'thin and tough', 'recio y fuerte', NULL),
(434, '-pïna#1', '-pïna', 'pina', 'pInA', 'suck', 'chupar', 'Evidence for the length of the vowels comes from modern dialects:\r\nLa Huasteca, Mecayapan, Pipil.\r\n'),
