(436, '-saka#2', '-zaca', 'çaca', 'sAkA', 'transport', 'transportar', NULL),
(144, '-säli', '-zäli', 'çali', 'sAlI', 'stick', 'pegarse', NULL),
(621, '-samä', '-zamä', 'çama', 'sAmA', 'swell', 'hincharse', 'Evidence for the length of the vowels comes from modern dialects:\r\nCoatepec C., La Huasteca, Mecayapan, Pipil, Zacapoaxtla.\r\n'),
(61, '-san', '-zan', 'çan', 'sAn', '', '', NULL),
(758, '-<sancto>', '-sancto', 'sancto', 'sAnctO', 'holy', 'santo', NULL),
(437, '-sawa#1', '-zahua', 'çahua', 'sAwA', 'fast', 'ayunar', NULL),
(802, '-sawa#2', '-zahua', 'çahua', 'sAwA', 'mange', 'sarna', 'In a number of modern dialects, Coatepec Costales, La Huasteca, Tetelcingo,\r\nZacapoaxtla, the first vowel is long. However, Carochi (fol.127r, p.531)\r\nspecifies it as short, \"Çáhuátl, ambas breues, sarna ò viruelas, o cosa\r\nsemejante. çähuätl, ambas largas, rio de Tlazcala.\" We chose to follow\r\nCarochi and mark it short.\r\n'),
(801, '-säyöl', '-zäyöl', 'çayol', 'sAyOl', 'fly', 'mosca', NULL),
(1151, '-sA¹A', '-zAcuA', 'çaqua', 'sA¹A', '?', '?', NULL),
(822, '-sa´¹än', '-za´cuän', 'çaquan', 'sA¹An', '\"bird\"', '\"pájaro\"', 'Evidence for glottal stop and length of the second vowel comes from Nahuatl\r\nspoken in La Huasteca today; according to Terrence Kaufman, it there has the\r\nform zähcuämëtl and means ´green jay´.\r\n'),
