(211, '-äxkä#1', '-äxcä', 'axca', 'AxkA', 'property', 'posesión', 'We have chosen the possessed form as the entry form for this word because\r\noccurrences of the absolute form are extremely rare, and because we are\r\nuncertain which one to choose.\r\nThe form äxcäitl is found in Carochi´s grammar (five occurences, one in a\r\nsentence), in Aldama y Guevara (1754), and in Clavijero (17??, 1974); these\r\nthree are all Jesuits, and in general the latter two have relied on Carochi.\r\nThe form is not found in the first Jesuit grammar, Antonio de Rincón´s from\r\n1595.\r\nThe form axcatl occurs in Molina´s Spanish-Nahuatl dictionary under\r\n´possession´, but not in the Nahuatl-Spanish part; and in the Florentine\r\nCodex there is at least one occurrence of this form, CF VI f. 178v.\r\nIn recent works on Nahuatl, the word has been classified with four other\r\nnouns ending in long vowel plus itl, namely cuäitl ´head´, cuëitl ´skirt´\r\nmäitl ´hand´, and töcäitl ´name´; in spite of the general rule in Nahuatl\r\nwhich shortens word final vowels, these four nouns all retain the length of\r\nthe long vowel in the possessed form, e.g. -cuä; Andrews (1975, p. 423), \r\nLauney (1979, p. 93 and 1986, p. 558); Karttunen, (1983, p. 14) give the\r\npossessed form of äxca with a long final vowel. However, there is no\r\nevidence supporting the assumption that the final vowel in the possessed\r\nform of -äxca was - or is - long:\r\n1) in Carochi´s grammar there are six occurrences with the final vowel\r\nunmarked for length;\r\n2) in writing about the very few long final vowels, \"son largas mas que las\r\nvocales finales de las palabras Castellanas, y ademas de ser largas se\r\npronuncian en tono vajo.. \" he does not mention äxca, f. 2v (p. 402);\r\n3) in the modern dialects I have not seen a single case of a long final\r\nvowel in the possessed form, Tetelcingo has ïmä (imö) ´his hand´ as opposed\r\nto ihuäxca (ihuöxca) ´his´ (Brewer and Brewer 1962), and in Ixhuatlancillo,\r\nVC, mäyitl ´hand´, nomä ´my hand´ is opposed to nowäxca ´mine´ (Kaufmann\r\n1979, pp. 10,60).\r\n4) a possessor noun is derived from the four nouns with the variant -e´,\r\nïx-cuäye´ (Mol)., cuëye´, mäye´ (Car. f. 55r, p.458), töcäye´ (Mol.);\r\nwhereas äxca takes the variant hua´, äxcahua´ (Car. f. 55r, p. 458 and\r\nMol).\r\nIn discussing the nouns ending in a long vowel plus itl, Launey says:\r\n\r\n          Il reste d´ailleurs quelques problèmes. Le premier est qu´äxcäitl\r\n     constitue un cas à part, puisque comme nous l´avons vu le nom possessif\r\n     correspondant est en -huâ et non en -ê, c´est-à-dire qu´il semble\r\n     supposer un radical à final vocalique; il y a d´ailleurs au moins un\r\n     exemple de la forme absolue äxcätl (si toutefois il ne s´agit pas\r\n     d´une erreur du copiste):\r\n\r\n          (288)(VI,213) in ömpa tipöuhqui, in ömpa titlatquitl, in ömpa\r\n          täxcätl \"toi qui appartiens à (litt. \"es recensé\") là-bas, qui\r\n          es une possession de là-bas, un bien de là-bas\"\r\n\r\n          Il est d´autre part tentant de rapprocher ce mot de äyi\r\n     (3.2.5.2.4) \"faire, être dans un certain état\", dont il pourrait être\r\n     le déverbal perfectif (5.2.3.4.3.7), et de supposer q´une forme qui\r\n     normalement devrait être *n-äx-ca \"ma situation\", d´où peut-être \"mes\r\n     richesses\" ait pu être refaite (ou maintenue) en n-äx-cä, la voyelle\r\n     longue finale étant rejustifié par une nouvelle forme absolue äxcäitl\r\n     sur le modèle no-mä / mäitl. Mais on voit évidemment mal pourquoi ce\r\n     mot serait le seul, de tous les déverbaux perfectifs, à avoir subi ce\r\n     traitement.\r\n', 9, 0, 0, NULL, '\r\n'),
